  "body": "## Overview\n\nFollow-up to #29869, for [this question](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/29869#issuecomment-5988697570).\n\nDepends on #30065, which is the first commit here. The MUL_MAT_ID change is the [second commit](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/30047/commits/c1d2e51bbeca8cb1d5b0e2cd312e1a3c23bda614). I will rebase once #30065 is merged.\n\n- MUL_MAT_ID gets MMA kernels on the same kernel bodies as the few-row MUL_MAT kernels, so it takes the same types. With many experts, a batch of 32 to 192 tokens gives each expert only a few tokens, and `kernel_mul_mm_id` pads every expert to 32. `kernel_mul_mm_id_map0` now also lists the experts that received tokens, and `kernel_mul_mv_mma_id_*` runs over the weight tiles of those experts, reading each tile once per 8 tokens.\n- MUL_MAT_ID picks its kernel by tokens per expert (`n_tokens*n_used/n_expert`). Below 2 (0.5 when K <= 512) it uses the mat-vec kernel and above that the MMA kernel. It switches to `mul_mm_id` past 6 tokens per expert (4 for F32, F16 and BF16) and from 256 tokens (128 when K <= 512).\n\n## Additional information\n\nApple M3 Ultra (60-core GPU), macOS 15.7.9. Speedups are the time on master divided by the time with this PR, from two interleaved runs of each build.\n\n`test-backend-ops perf -o MUL_MAT_ID` on the Qwen3.6-35B-A3B expert shapes (256 experts, 8 used, Q4_K):\n\n| tokens | 16 | 24 | 32 | 48 | 64 | 96 | 128 | 192 | 256 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| gate/up (m=512, k=2048) | 1.00x | 1.00x | 2.31x | 1.82x | 1.75x | 1.72x | 1.67x | 1.47x | 0.99x |\n| down (m=2048, k=512) | 1.06x | 1.21x | 1.56x | 1.55x | 1.55x | 1.53x | 1.00x | 1.00x | 1.00x |\n\nOn the 128/8, 64/8, 32/4 and 8/2 expert shapes with Q4_0, Q4_K, Q6_K, Q8_0, IQ2_XS, IQ4_XS, MXFP4, F16, BF16 and F32, the speedup is 1.03x to 2.73x wherever the kernel changes. Where both builds run the same kernel it stays between 0.94x and 1.08x, the same spread as between two builds of master.\n\n`llama-batched-bench -npp 512 -ntg 32` with Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q4_K_M, text generation. Each build ran three times in a row, and the table shows the mean of runs 2 and 3, after the shader cache was warm:\n\n| sequences | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 8 | 16 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| master (t/s) | 95.0 | 146.1 | 184.6 | 213.1 | 297.4 | 346.3 |\n| this PR (t/s) | 95.6 | 145.5 | 185.1 | 213.5 | 297.1 | 372.0 |\n| speedup | 1.01x | 1.00x | 1.00x | 1.00x | 1.00x | 1.07x |\n\nAt 16 sequences the down projection gets 0.5 tokens per expert, which runs on the new kernel. Prompt processing is the same on both builds, and Qwen3.8-27B Q4_0 (dense) is within 0.2% at every batch size.\n\n- `llama-perplexity -ub 64` over 40 chunks, where the expert mat-muls get 2 tokens per expert and run on the new kernel: 5.8659 on master, 5.8620 with this PR.\n- Serial decoding and the DFlash-2 verify step (8 tokens, 0.25 per expert) stay on the mat-vec kernel and run at the same speed as on master.\n- With a cold shader cache, the `mul_mv_mma` library takes 1.2 s to compile instead of 0.48 s.\n\nTests:\n\n- `test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT,MUL_MAT_ID -b MTL0`: 3884/3884 on the M3 Ultra. On an Apple M5 with the tensor API on and off (`GGML_METAL_TENSOR_DISABLE=1`): MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ADD 2587/2587, MUL_MAT_ID 1437/1437.\n- The new MUL_MAT and MUL_MAT_ID cases also pass on CUDA (RTX 5090, GB10) and Vulkan (RTX 5090, GB10, Radeon 8060S).\n\n## Requirements\n\n- I have read and agree with the [contributing guidelines](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- AI usage disclosure: YES - the kernels, the tests, the benchmarks and this description were written with Claude Code (Anthropic).\n",
